{"statement_verdicts":[],"assumption_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"ass:lemma-lip-condition-regression","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Under the null-set convention for regression functions, the NL means: there are versions f of E_nu[Y|T] and fhat of E_mu[Ŷ|T̂] that are L-Lipschitz. The Lean says exactly this. LipschitzWith L for f and fhat, together with (f∘first coordinate) =ᵐ[nu] nu[second coordinate | comap of the first coordinate] and the same under mu for fhat, makes f and fhat versions of the conditional expectations under the laws of (T,Y) and (T̂,Ŷ). The comap uses the Borel structure on ℝ. Lipschitz on ℝ loses nothing against Lipschitz on the support: a McShane extension keeps the constant. Measurable f and Measurable fhat are redundant, since Lipschitz functions are continuous. Integrability and σ-finiteness of the trimmed measure, so that the conditional expectation is not Mathlib's junk value, are supplied at the consumer (regression_gap_le_wasserstein in Helpers/RegressionComparison.lean:180) by ProbabilityMeasure together with the pinned box-support condition ass:bk-owner-3 (RegressionLawsSupportedInBox). Both directions hold."},{"obj_id":"ass:lemma-lip-condition-bounded","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"The Lean `∀ t, |f t| ≤ K ∧ |fhat t| ≤ K` is the NL bound |f(t)| ≤ K and |f̂(t)| ≤ K, on the same versions f and fhat that the Lipschitz predicate constrains. A bound on all of ℝ rather than on the support is not narrower when taken jointly with the Lipschitz assumption. Clipping to [-K,K] is 1-Lipschitz, so it keeps a Lipschitz version Lipschitz with constant L. It also keeps the a.e. version property, because under the pinned support condition the regression functions take values in [-K,K] almost surely. If K is negative the predicate is unsatisfiable, exactly as the paper's bound would be, and the consumer additionally has 0<K. The two are equivalent."},{"obj_id":"ass:lemma-combin-err-weights","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"MixtureWeights p means p ∈ stdSimplex ℝ (Fin n): every p_k ≥ 0 and Σ p_k = 1. The NL has p_k ∈ [0,1] and Σ p_k = 1. The two are equivalent, since nonnegative terms summing to 1 are each at most 1. The pinned readings ass:bk-1 (finite first moments) and ass:pin-75 (each μ_k and μ̂_k is a probability measure) are separate conditions of lem:combin-err about the measures, not about the weights, so this predicate does not need to carry them."},{"obj_id":"ass:lemma-gumbel-err-parameters","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"`0 < tau ∧ tau < 1 ∧ ∀ k, 0 < p k` is literally the NL '1 > τ > 0 and p_1,…,p_n > 0'. The pinned readings ass:pin-2 (the event E_δ) and ass:pin-165 (X^τ, μ^τ and the limit X^0) concern other objects in the lemma, not these parameters. The two are equivalent."},{"obj_id":"ass:lemma-sup-wasserstein-pseudodimension","verdict":"faithful-refinement","note":"Pinned reading ass:pin-59: for every coordinate j, Pdim({x ↦ f_j(x) : f ∈ F}) < d_F. The Lean asks, for every j : Fin d, for a natural number k with k < dF and HasPseudoDimAtMost(coordinate-j class, k). HasPseudoDimAtMost (Causalean VC/Basic.lean:36) is the standard pseudo-dimension bound: the VC dimension of the subgraph classifiers, traced on every finite sample in X×ℝ, is at most k. Indexing the class by the subtype F does not matter, because shattering depends only on the range. Equivalence: if Pdim ≤ k < dF then Pdim < dF; conversely take k = Pdim, which is a finite natural number below dF. The extra conjunct 0 ≤ dF follows from the rest when d ≥ 1 (0 ≤ k < dF). It adds content only in the degenerate case d = 0, where it records that d_F is a nonnegative bound on a dimension. That is the intrinsic domain of d_F, so it is not a narrowing of the paper's models."}],"substrate_gates":[],"escalate":null}

