{"statement_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"def:hilbert-partitions","verdict":"drift","note":"Lean and paper do not have the same extension: `Box` only requires `lower j ≤ upper j`, so cells may be degenerate. Paper ⟹ Lean holds for full-dimensional closed boxes with disjoint interiors (pin-164): such a box is the closure of its interior, so intersections lie in both frontiers. Lean ⟹ paper fails. Counterexample with d=1: at every level take [0,1] together with the point {1}. All Lean fields hold (zero, union-of-children, box carriers, and {1} ⊆ frontier ∩ frontier), but nothing obtained by cutting [0,1]^d with coordinate planes looks like this. The reverse direction also fails if degenerate cells are allowed in the paper: [0,1] with {1/2} has disjoint interiors but violates `boundaryIntersections`. The NL's intent is clear, since cutting a cube with planes gives full-dimensional pieces. FIX: in `coordinateCuts` require `∃ b : Box d, (∀ j, b.lower j < b.upper j) ∧ cells n w = b.carrier`, or make `Box` strict. With that change, `boundaryIntersections` is equivalent to the pinned 'pairwise disjoint interiors, may share boundaries', and the rest matches: C_0 is the cube; each word has 2^d children whose union is the parent; cells are closed coordinate cells covering the cube."},{"obj_id":"def:cor-rate-objective","verdict":"drift","note":"`rateObjective (Y : Joint kind → ℝ) := Y` is the identity on an arbitrary function, so the anchor's extension is every real function of V. The paper's F is one specific object: F(V)=Y, the projection onto the scalar outcome coordinate (pin-152). The def also omits μ_T=Unif([t1,t2]) and ε>0. The pinning is supplied only by consumers: TRate.lean writes `rateObjective (scalarOutcome Y hY)` and `uniformIntervention t1 t2 ht12`, and the Prop at Setup/Assumptions/Conventions.lean:399 pins both, so the theorems are fine but the def node is not realized by its own declaration. FIX (mechanical): define `rateObjective (Y : Fin n) (hY : valueDim (kind Y) =