{"statement_verdicts":[{"obj_id":"lem:hilbert","verdict":"matched","note":"The Lean statement and the frozen node imply each other once O_n is re-encoded. The Lean indexes O_n by dyadic lattice coordinates a : Fin d → Fin (2^n) instead of words i_1…i_n. This is a faithful reparameterization. For d≥1, hdyadic says range(cells n) = dyadicCubeCollection d n, which is exactly the displayed grid. With 2^{dn} words and 2^{dn} distinct dyadic cubes, cells n is a bijection from words onto cubes. Each child cells(n+1)(childWord w j) is a level-(n+1) dyadic cube inside cells n w, by HasPropertyStar.refinement. It must be a geometric child, and covering the parent forces all 2^d children to appear, using their centers. Here childWord = Fin.lastCases, so it appends the last digit. So the word prefix relation holds iff the cubes are geometric parent and child, iff (O(n+1) i r)/2 = (O n j r) for every r. Translation: O'_n(i) := (cells n)^{-1}(dyadicGridCube n (O n i)), and conversely O n i := the coordinates of cells n (O' n i). Both are bijections, as pin c85b9186 requires. Conclusion 1: the 0-indexed i/2^d = j is the paper's 1-indexed (j-1)2^d < i ≤ j·2^d. Conclusion 2: j = i+1 inside Fin(2^{dn}) is the pinned range 1≤i<2^{nd} (pin 0bcb2cef). The face {x | x k = a, x r ∈ [l r, u r] with u r − l r = 2^{-n} for r≠k} is exactly a (d−1)-dimensional cube. Two same-level dyadic cubes meet in a product of full intervals and points, so a (d−1)-cube intersection must have sides 2^{-n}; at d=1 it is a point. The first conjunct (range equality) follows automatically from O being a bijection plus hdyadic. For d=0 both sides hold trivially: cells n = univ by zero and refinement, there is a single word, and conclusion 2 is vacuous."}],"assumption_verdicts":[],"substrate_gates":[{"name":"Papers.TbsNeuralCausalPi.hilbert_ordering","statement":"For partitions with property (*) that are the standard closed dyadic grid when d≥1, there exist bijective orderings O_n : Fin(2^{dn}) ≃ dyadic lattice addresses such that (1) the level-(n+1) cells i with i/2^d = j are children of O_n(j), and (2) consecutive cells meet in a nonempty (d−1)-dimensional face with sides 2^{-n}.","classical_fact":"The Hilbert-curve ordering of the dyadic grid is nested (each parent's 2^d children are consecutive) and consecutive cells share a face (consecutive lattice points differ by 1 in one coordinate), as produced by the Hilbert index construction of Hamilton & Rau-Chaplin (2007), Algorithm 3.","missing_infra":"","gate_class":"cited","source":{"cite_id":"cite:hamilton2007compact","locator":"Algorithm 3"},"check_status":"cited-verified-attested"}],"escalate":null}

